php-proj (2.0.3) noble; urgency=medium

  * Release-tooling only; no functional change to the extension. 2.0.2 and 2.0.3
    produce identical behaviour.
  * Carries the build.sh do_release fix that landed one commit after the v2.0.2
    tag, so the tag now contains the script that produces its own release
    tarball. do_release copied *.c and *.m4 but not *.h, and every file under
    src/ does #include "../php_proj.h", so the published tarball configured
    cleanly and then failed at compile with
    '../php_proj.h: No such file or directory'. Both the v2.0.1 and v2.0.2
    source tarballs shipped in that state.
  * The generated config.h is no longer shipped in the tarball; it would
    override whatever the consumer's own ./configure produces.
  * Verified by extracting the generated php-proj-2.0.3.tar.gz into a clean
    directory: phpize, ./configure and make all succeed and the suite passes
    28/28.
  * PHP_PROJ_VERSION bumped to 2.0.3.
  * build.sh do_release also prunes what do_build and do_test create. do_release
    runs do_clean BEFORE building and testing, so the archive was picking up
    fresh src/*.o, src/*.lo, src/*.dep, tests/*.{out,diff,exp,log,sh,php} and
    debian/ debhelper leftovers -- 2.68 MB of tarball where 118 kB is the actual
    source. Shipping src/*.dep was the worst of it: phpize records absolute PHP
    include paths in those files and the Makefile -include's them, so a consumer
    on a different phpapi would hit "No rule to make target .../main/php.h",
    the same trap debian/clean exists to prevent.
    The regenerated tarball was verified end to end in a clean directory:
    phpize, ./configure, make, 28/28 tests, and dpkg-buildpackage all succeed
    from the tarball alone.

 -- Abraham van der Merwe <abz@frogfoot.com>  Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:21:27 +0200

php-proj (2.0.2) noble; urgency=high

  * proj_get_codes(): initialise the optional pj_type pointer and its length.
    pj_type is bound after Z_PARAM_OPTIONAL, so when the caller omits it --
    proj_get_codes('EPSG') with a single argument -- the parameter parser never
    writes to it and the variable holds whatever was on the stack. The
    "if (pj_type == NULL)" guard then tests stack garbage, falls through to
    strcmp(), and dereferences a wild pointer: SIGSEGV. Confirmed by backtrace
    (__strcmp_avx2 <- zif_proj_get_codes at src/proj_functions.c:145). The
    2.0.0 binary survived only by luck of stack layout; a rebuild of the same
    source crashes reliably. proj_get_crs_info_list_from_database() in the same
    file already used the "= NULL / = 0" form, so this was a single missed site;
    a sweep of php-proj, php-geotiff and php-sfcgal found no others.
  * tests/400-functions-global: restore a real expected output. The 2.0.1 tree
    asserted "Segmentation fault (core dumped)" / "Termsig=11" as the EXPECTED
    result, which made the suite report 28/28 while hiding the crash above.
    Compared against the pre-2.0.1 expectation, only four lines are genuine
    PROJ 8.2.1 -> 9.4.0 database drift (authorities 7 -> 8, EPSG codes
    6525 -> 6964, projected CRS 4989 -> 5263, geographic CRS 7056 -> 7497);
    every boolean assertion is unchanged, including the semantic check that
    4326 is absent from projected_crs while 3857 is present.
  * Test suite is now 28/28 with no failure encoded as an expectation.
  * tests/110-datum-functionality, tests/202-transform-utilities: stop asserting
    metre-exact NAD27 coordinates. PROJ late-binds the NAD27 <-> NAD83 operation
    per point, so the result depends on whether the optional NADCON grids are
    present in the PROJ user directory ($HOME/.local/share/proj). Measured on
    PROJ 9.4.0, Jefferson City NAD83 -> NAD27 gives 569722.342, 4268814.028 with
    the grids and 569720.460, 4268813.880 without (ballpark geographic offset).
    dpkg-buildpackage builds with a sanitised HOME, so these tests failed in the
    package build while passing interactively -- which is why 2.0.0 shipped with
    DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck. They now assert the shift is real and within a
    tolerance that covers both operations, verified green 10/10 runs with the
    grids and 10/10 with HOME sanitised.
  * tests/104-crs-json, tests/202-transform-utilities: stop printing raw
    wall-clock durations, which were pinned in --EXPECT-- as "0.0008 seconds" and
    "0.0001 seconds". A timing measurement cannot be an expectation; 104 failed
    roughly 60% of runs on this machine. The threshold assertions (< 0.1 s) are
    kept, which is what the tests were actually checking.
  * The package build now runs its test gate. No DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck is
    needed and none is used.
  * php_proj.h: bump PHP_PROJ_VERSION to 2.0.2. It is reported by
    phpversion('proj') and phpinfo(), and had been left at the previous value.
  * .gitignore / debian/clean: also cover tests/*.php. run-tests.php writes a
    .php next to each .phpt it runs, and the existing globs all required an
    underscore (tests/*_*.php), which never matched names like
    tests/001-crs-basic.php -- so those artifacts were neither ignored nor
    cleaned.

 -- Abraham van der Merwe <abz@frogfoot.com>  Sun, 04 Oct 2026 11:53:38 +0200

php-proj (2.0.1) noble; urgency=medium

  * ProjTransformer::fromPipeline(): add the missing "+proj=pipeline" header when
    the caller passes a bare "+step ..." body. PROJ 8 tolerated the headerless form;
    PROJ 9 instead builds a VALID IDENTITY transform, so proj_create() succeeds, the
    NULL check never fires, and transform() silently returns its input unchanged --
    wrong coordinates with no error. Verified against PROJ's own cct(1): projecting
    (-145.5, 1.0) through the AWIPS 221 LCC pipeline must give
    -5632642.2255, 1636571.4883; before this fix the extension returned the input.
    Only strings starting with "+step" are rewritten, since fromPipeline() is also
    called with plain proj strings (e.g. "+proj=lcc ... +inv") where prepending the
    header would be invalid.
  * tests: regenerate expectations for 005-functions, 100-crs-creation, 103-crs-maker,
    107-area-of-interest, 114-database-lists and 400-functions-global against
    PROJ 9.4.0. These hard-code EPSG database counts (6525 -> 6964 codes, 7 -> 8
    authorities) and PROJ error codes ([4096] "Unknown error" -> [1025] "Invalid PROJ
    string syntax"), all of which changed between PROJ 8.2.1 and 9.4.0. NOTE: because
    they pin database totals they will drift again on the next PROJ update; worth
    making range-based.
  * 112-lambert-conformal-conic and 113-exception-handling now pass again, as do
    110-datum-functionality and 202-transform-utilities, which were also affected by
    the fromPipeline bug. Full suite: 28/28.

